    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Kyiv was the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus'</a> from the late 9th century, reached its height in the middle of the 11th century under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaroslav-the-wise">Yaroslav the Wise</a>, and was left in ruins by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> on 6 December 1240.</li><li>For centuries afterwards it was a provincial city under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, yet it remained the seat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> metropolitan and a centre of Ukrainian culture.</li><li>It became the capital of short-lived Ukrainian states in 1917, of Soviet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> in 1934 and of independent Ukraine in 1991, and it suffered greatly in the famine of 1932 and 1933 and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Between 1146 and 1246 Kyiv changed hands 47 times and was ruled by 24 princes, 35 of whose tenures lasted less than a year.</p></aside>
    </section>

