    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">c. 482</span><span class="rf-text">The traditional date of the city's founding, celebrated as its 1,500th anniversary in 1982</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">988</span><span class="rf-text">Prince Volodymyr I accepts <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> and orders the people of Kyiv to be baptised in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dnieper">Dnieper</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1169</span><span class="rf-text">An army of eleven princes sent by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andrei-bogolyubsky">Andrei Bogolyubsky</a> sacks the city for three days</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1240</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batu-khan">Batu Khan</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> besiege Kyiv and destroy it</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1362</span><span class="rf-text">After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-blue-waters">Battle of Blue Waters</a> Kyiv passes to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1667</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/truce-of-andrusovo">Truce of Andrusovo</a> cedes Kyiv to the Tsardom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1941</span><span class="rf-text">Nazi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> occupies the city, and nearly 34,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> are killed at Babi Yar</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1991</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> declares independence in Kyiv, which becomes the capital of an independent state</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/kyi-shchek-and-khoryv"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kyi-shchek-and-khoryv.jpg" alt="Kyi, Shchek and Khoryv" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/kyi-shchek-and-khoryv">Kyi, Shchek and Khoryv</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kyi,_Czech,_Khoryv_and_Lubed_in_der_Radziwi%C5%82%C5%82chronik.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Kyiv stands on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dnieper">Dnieper</a>, on the hills of its western bank. The first known humans in the region lived there in the late Palaeolithic, and the people of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> belonged to the Trypillian culture, whose artefacts have been found in the area. In the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> tribes who farmed, kept livestock and traded with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scythians">Scythians</a> and the states of the northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> coast settled around Kyiv. Finds of Roman coins of the 2nd to 4th centuries suggest trade with the eastern provinces of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a>.</p><p>Scholars continue to debate when the city was founded. The traditional date is 482 CE, so the city celebrated its 1,500th anniversary in 1982. Archaeological data indicates the 6th or 7th century, and some researchers date the founding as late as the late 9th century. Tree-ring analysis of log dwellings in Podil has found evidence of settlement only as far back as 887, and Omeljan Pritsak writes that Kyiv did not exist as a town before the last quarter of the 9th century.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/primary-chronicle">Primary Chronicle</a> tells that the Polyanian brothers <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyi-shchek-and-khoryv">Kyi, Shchek and Khoryv</a> and their sister Lybid founded the town on three hills and named it after Kyi. Another legend holds that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andrew-the-apostle">Saint Andrew</a> passed through the area in the 1st century and erected a cross where a church was later built. The earliest more reliable evidence suggests an early medieval Slavic settlement that paid tribute to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khazar-khaganate">Khazars</a>, on the route between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scandinavia">Scandinavia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="growth">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Growth &amp; Great Ages</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/yaroslav-the-wise"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yaroslav-the-wise.jpg" alt="Yaroslav the Wise" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/yaroslav-the-wise">Yaroslav the Wise</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:YaroslavWiseSeal.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>As the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus'</a> the city stood on the trade route between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varangians">Varangians</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a>. In 968 it withstood a siege by the nomadic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pechenegs">Pechenegs</a>. By 1000 CE it had a population of 45,000, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thietmar-of-merseburg">Thietmar of Merseburg</a>, describing it in his chronicle from 1017, recorded over 400 churches and 8 markets. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaroslav-the-wise">Yaroslav the Wise</a>, Grand Prince from 1019 to 1054, built St Sophia Cathedral, and the middle of the 11th century was the city's political and cultural height. An Armenian merchant factory was based there from the 11th century until the Mongol invasions.</p><p>Kyiv had a reputation as one of the largest cities in the world. Before the Mongol invasion its population was about 35,000 to 40,000 according to Orest Subtelny, and 36,000 to 50,000 at the end of the 12th century according to Janet L. B. Martin.</p><p>After 1240 Kyiv was for centuries a provincial capital of marginal importance, though it remained the seat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> metropolitan. In the early 17th century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv-pechersk-lavra">Monastery of the Caves</a> became a centre of Orthodox cultural revival, and in 1632 Peter Mogila founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv-mohyla-academy">Kyiv-Mohyla Academy</a>. In the late 19th century, during the Russian industrial revolution, Kyiv became a trade and transport centre that exported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> and grain by railway and on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dnieper">Dnieper</a>. By 1900 it had a population of 250,000, and in 1892 the first electric tram line of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> had opened there.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Kyiv&#8221; cites where it covers Golden Horde period, Historical population, Ethnic composition, Jews:</p><ul><li>Janet Martin, Medieval Russia: 980–1584. Second Edition. E-book (Cambridge University Press, 2007)</li><li>Irene Rima Makaryk, Modernism in Kyiv: Kiev/Kyïv/Kiev/Kijów: Jubilant Experimentation (University of Toronto Press, 2010)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rulers">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Who Ruled It</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/oleg-of-novgorod"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/oleg-of-novgorod.jpg" alt="Oleg of Novgorod" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/oleg-of-novgorod">Oleg of Novgorod</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D0%9F%D1%80%D0%B8%D0%BD%D0%BE%D1%88%D0%B5%D0%BD%D0%B8%D0%B5_%D0%BA%D0%BD%D1%8F%D0%B7%D1%8E_%D0%9E%D0%BB%D0%B5%D0%B3%D1%83_%D0%B4%D0%B0%D0%BD%D0%B8_%D0%BF%D0%BE%D0%BA%D0%BE%D1%80%D0%B5%D0%BD%D0%BD%D1%8B%D0%BC%D0%B8_%D0%BD%D0%B0%D1%80%D0%BE%D0%B4%D0%B0%D0%BC%D0%B8.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/primary-chronicle">Primary Chronicle</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oleg-of-novgorod">Oleg the Wise</a> conquered Kyiv in 881 or 882. Omeljan Pritsak and Constantine Zuckerman dispute this and hold that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khazar-khaganate">Khazar</a> rule continued as late as the 920s, and other historians suggest that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magyars">Magyar</a> tribes ruled the city between 840 and 878. From Oleg's seizure Kyiv was the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus'</a>, ruled by the Varangian Rurikid dynasty, which gradually became Slavicised. The inner Principality of Kyiv emerged in 1132. After 1240 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a> ruled the city through yarlyks issued by Mongol rulers. In 1362 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algirdas">Algirdas</a> defeated a Golden Horde army at Blue Waters and took Kyiv into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania</a>. In 1471 the principality was abolished and a voivodeship set up, and in 1569, under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/union-of-lublin">Union of Lublin</a>, the city passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crown-of-the-kingdom-of-poland">Crown of the Kingdom of Poland</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohdan-khmelnytsky">Bohdan Khmelnytsky</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossacks</a> entered the city during their uprising. By the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/truce-of-andrusovo">Truce of Andrusovo</a> of 31 January 1667 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth</a> ceded Kyiv to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, on paper for two years, and the Eternal Peace of 1686 acknowledged the existing situation. None of the Polish-Russian treaties concerning the city has been ratified. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catherine-the-great">Catherine the Great</a> abolished <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>'s autonomy in 1775.</p><p>In 1917 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-rada">Central Rada</a> was established in Kyiv, and on 7 November 1917 it proclaimed the Ukrainian People's Republic with its capital there. The city changed hands sixteen times from the end of 1918 to August 1920. It was part of the Ukrainian Soviet Socialist Republic, its capital from 1934, until 1991.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Kyiv&#8221; cites where it covers Golden Horde period, Lithuanian and Polish period, Cossack period and Russian suzerainty, Soviet era:</p><ul><li>Paul Robert Magocsi, A History of Ukraine: The Land and Its Peoples (University of Toronto Press, 1996)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="life">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Life in the City</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/sigismund-ii-augustus"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sigismund-ii-augustus.jpg" alt="Sigismund II Augustus" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/sigismund-ii-augustus">Sigismund II Augustus</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cranach_the_Younger_Sigismund_II_Augustus.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Lucas Cranach the Younger, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>From the late 9th century Kyiv mixed pagan Slavic, Norse, Christian, Islamic and Jewish traditions. In 988 Prince Volodymyr I ordered the residents to accept baptism in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dnieper">Dnieper</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> became dominant, with Church Slavonic as the literary standard. Under Lithuanian rule the core of the city lay in Podil, and a castle with 18 towers stood on Zamkova Hora. From 1494 the city's autonomy under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magdeburg">Magdeburg</a> rights grew, and a charter of 1516 finished the process. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsar">Tsar</a> Nicholas I abolished the Magdeburg law on 23 December 1834, the year St Vladimir University was founded.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> are first mentioned in Kyiv in a 10th-century letter. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigismund-ii-augustus">Sigismund II Augustus</a> granted them equal rights in the 16th century. They were expelled in 1654 and probably could not settle again until the early 1790s, and in 1836 the Pale of Settlement banned them from the city, though a large Jewish community emerged in the 19th century. On the eve of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> there were over 81,000 Jews in Kyiv. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a> were an important element in the 19th century. The Russian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> of 1897 recorded about 240,000 people, of whom about 56% spoke Russian, 23% Ukrainian and 7% Polish. Tsar Alexis expelled the local <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a> in 1660.</p><p>The names of the city's districts follow its relief: Podil means lower, Pechersk means caves. The Left Bank on the east side of the Dnieper was built up only in the 20th century.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="crises">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Sieges &amp; Disasters</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/batu-khan"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/batu-khan.jpg" alt="Batu Khan" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/batu-khan">Batu Khan</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Batu_Khan_on_the_Throne_by_Rashid_al-Din_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Rashid al-Din Hamadani, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In 1169 an army of eleven princes sent by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andrei-bogolyubsky">Andrei Bogolyubsky</a> sacked Kyiv for three days and left the old town and the prince's hall in ruins. In 1203 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rurik-rostislavich">Rurik Rostislavich</a> and his Kipchak allies captured and burned it, and in the 1230s Rus' princes besieged and ravaged it several times. On 6 December 1240 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batu-khan">Batu Khan</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> besieged the city and destroyed it. According to one account the garrison had thrown down the bodies of the Mongol envoys, and in revenge the Mongols broke down the gates, slaughtered much of the population and razed the city. In 1482 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimean-tatars">Crimean Tatars</a> sacked and burned much of it, and in 1811 the Great Fire of Podil devastated it.</p><p>In the 1930s Kyiv suffered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holodomor">Holodomor</a> of 1932 and 1933 and the purges, with mass executions at Babi Yar and in the Bykivnia forest. In the Battle of Kyiv in 1941 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axis-powers">Axis</a> killed or captured more than 600,000 Soviet soldiers. German occupation lasted from 19 September 1941 to 6 November 1943. Soon after it began, mines or explosives set by the Soviet side destroyed most of the buildings on the Khreshchatyk, which burned for days. On 29 and 30 September 1941 nearly 34,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> of Kyiv were massacred at Babi Yar, and it is estimated that the Germans killed more than 100,000 people there during the war. A pogrom in September 1945 left five Jews dead. The Chernobyl accident of 1986 occurred about 100 km north of the city.</p><p>In February 2022 Russian forces attempted to seize Kyiv and were repelled on its outskirts. The Russian offensive in Kyiv Oblast was defeated on 2 April 2022, and air strikes on the city have continued since.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Kyiv&#8221; cites where it covers Cossack period and Russian suzerainty:</p><ul><li>Paul Robert Magocsi, A History of Ukraine: The Land and Its Peoples (University of Toronto Press, 1996)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/taras-shevchenko"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/taras-shevchenko.jpg" alt="Taras Shevchenko" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/taras-shevchenko">Taras Shevchenko</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Т._Г._Шевченко._Квітень_1859.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Andrey Denyer, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Cathedral of St Sophia and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv-pechersk-lavra">Kyiv Pechersk Lavra</a> are recognised by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a>, and in September 2023 the World Heritage Committee placed them on the List of World Heritage in Danger because of the war. Other landmarks include the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-gate-bhaktapur">Golden Gate</a>, the Mariinskyi Palace, built from 1745 to 1752, and the Brodsky Choral Synagogue. Kyiv was a cradle of the Christianisation of Rus' and remained a centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodoxy">Eastern Orthodoxy</a>, a pilgrimage destination for centuries. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv-mohyla-academy">Kyiv-Mohyla Academy</a> is the oldest of the city's universities, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taras-shevchenko">Shevchenko</a> University, founded in 1834, is the oldest in continuous operation.</p><p>Kyiv has been a centre of invention and of Ukrainian public life. Igor Sikorsky, born there, built and tested the world's first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helicopter">helicopter</a> in the city. The Ukrainian parliament proclaimed the Declaration of Independence in Kyiv on 24 August 1991. Mass demonstrations in support of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orange-revolution">Orange Revolution</a> followed in 2004 and 2005, and from November 2013 to February 2014 central Kyiv was the main location of Euromaidan, also called the Revolution of Dignity.</p><p>Kyiv is the official romanised Ukrainian spelling, while Kiev is the traditional English name. The city had 2,611,327 people at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> of 2001, of whom 82.2% were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukrainians">Ukrainians</a>, and has nearly three million today.</p></div><div class="profile-inv"><p class="profile-inv-k">Songs in history</p><p class="profile-inv-l"><a href="/songs/national-anthem-of-ukraine">State Anthem of Ukraine<span>1862</span></a><a href="/songs/oi-u-luzi-chervona-kalyna">Oh, the Red Viburnum in the Meadow<span>c. 1914</span></a><a href="/songs/shchedryk">Shchedryk<span>1916</span></a></p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Kyiv, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dnieper">Dnieper</a>, was the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus'</a> and was destroyed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> in 1240. It later served as a provincial city under several powers, then as the capital of Soviet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> from 1934 and of independent Ukraine from 1991.</em></p>

